### When should you NOT use Optional?

### Rule 1: Return empty collection, not Optional<Collection>

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class EmptyCollectionRule {
    record Transaction(String id) {}

    // WRONG: Optional<List<T>>
    static Optional<List<Transaction>> findBad(String accountId) {
        List<Transaction> txns = List.of(); // empty
        return txns.isEmpty() ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(txns);
    }

    // CORRECT: return empty list directly
    static List<Transaction> findGood(String accountId) {
        return List.of(); // empty list = no results
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Bad: caller must unwrap
        findBad("SAV2001").orElse(List.of()).forEach(System.out::println);

        // Good: caller just iterates
        findGood("SAV2001").forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

### Rule 2: Use primitive OptionalInt/Long/Double

```java
import java.util.OptionalInt;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

// WRONG: Optional<Integer> with autoboxing
// Optional<Integer> max = stream.map(...).max(Integer::compareTo);

// CORRECT: OptionalInt without boxing
OptionalInt max = IntStream.of(85, 92, 78, 95, 88).max();
max.ifPresent(score -> System.out.println("Max score: " + score));
```

### Rule 3: Avoid Optional in hot paths

```java
// WRONG: millions of Optional allocations
for (int i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {
    Optional<String> opt = Optional.ofNullable(cache.get(i)); // heap allocation!
    String val = opt.orElse("default");
}

// CORRECT: plain null check in hot path
for (int i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {
    String val = cache.get(i);
    if (val == null) val = "default"; // zero allocation
}
```

### Why does Optional allocate on the heap?

Every `Optional.of(value)` creates a new object on the heap (16 bytes header + reference). In tight loops processing millions of elements, this adds significant GC pressure. Plain null checks use zero allocation.

### When is Optional appropriate?

| Context | Use Optional? | Why |
|---------|:---:|-----|
| Service method return type | Yes | Signals 'may be absent' |
| Repository method return type | Yes | findById may not find |
| Inside a tight loop | No | Heap allocation overhead |
| Collection result | No | Return empty collection |
| Primitive value | No | Use OptionalInt/Long/Double |
| Field storage | No | Not Serializable |
| Method parameter | No | Awkward API |

### What about Optional with primitive wrappers?

```java
// WRONG: Optional<Integer> boxes twice (Integer wrapper + Optional wrapper)
Optional<Integer> opt = Optional.of(42); // Integer boxing + Optional allocation

// CORRECT: OptionalInt avoids both
OptionalInt opt = OptionalInt.of(42); // single primitive-aware allocation

// OptionalInt API:
optInt.isPresent();           // boolean
optInt.getAsInt();            // int (not get())
optInt.orElse(0);             // int default
optInt.ifPresent(i -> ...);   // IntConsumer
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: Optional<List<T>>
Optional<List<Account>> accounts = repository.findByBranch("Mumbai");
// Caller: accounts.orElse(List.of()).forEach(...) – awkward!

// CORRECT: return empty list
List<Account> accounts = repository.findByBranch("Mumbai");
// Caller: accounts.forEach(...) – clean!

// WRONG: Optional in a loop
for (Account a : accounts) {
    Optional.ofNullable(a.getEmail()).ifPresent(this::sendEmail);
}

// CORRECT: plain null check in a loop
for (Account a : accounts) {
    if (a.getEmail() != null) sendEmail(a.getEmail());
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Optional is designed for API clarity at method boundaries, not for replacing every null check in computation.
- Use Optional at service boundaries (return types). Use plain values inside tight algorithms and performance-critical code.
- Valhalla (Project Valhalla) may make Optional a value type in the future, eliminating heap allocation. Until then, avoid it in hot paths.
- IntStream.max() returns OptionalInt directly; wrapping in Optional<Integer> creates unnecessary boxing.
